--TEST--
Every `Rows` and `Row` reader is reached through a declared parameter, so where the value came from does not decide what it can answer — and a `?Row` is not a `Row` — `rule:core-classes/db-column-types`, spec § 18
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The agreement question: a result set and a row are *types*, so the two
// rosters below are reachable on a parameter that names nothing about the
// statement, the connection or the call that produced the value. That is the
// difference from `fetch`, where what a value can answer depended on the mode
// the fetch was asked with, so a helper taking "a row" had to be told which
// kind of row it was getting.
//
// `Rows` is generic at what its rows hydrated into (spec § 18's Results table),
// so the declared type writes that argument: `query` answers `Rows<Row>`, and
// there is no bare spelling that leaves it open — the same rule every `Core`
// generic follows.
class Reads {
    public static function set(Core\Db\Rows<Core\Db\Row> $rows): uint {
        var $all = $rows->all();
        var $head = $rows->first();
        var $one = $rows->value();
        var $byPosition = $rows->column(0);
        return $rows->count();
    }

    public static function row(Core\Db\Row $row): bool {
        var $whole = $row->toArray();
        var $any = $row->get("owner");
        var $owner = $row->string("owner");
        var $photo = $row->bytes("photo");
        var $id = $row->int("id");
        var $seq = $row->uint("seq");
        var $ratio = $row->float("ratio");
        var $active = $row->bool("active");
        var $balance = $row->decimal("balance");
        var $seen = $row->instant("seen_at");
        var $born = $row->date("born_on");
        var $opens = $row->time("opens_at");
        var $external = $row->uuid("external_id");
        return $row->has("owner");
    }
}

var $db = Core\Db::connect("main");
var $rows = $db->query("select * from accounts", []);

// Both helpers, over the values `query` and `all` hand back.
var $counted = Reads::set($rows);
foreach ($rows->all() as Core\Db\Row $each) {
    var $asked = Reads::row($each);
}

// And the one refusal, which is the same nullability the assignment cases pin,
// asserted at an argument instead: `first` answers `?Row`, the parameter says
// `Row`, and there is no coercion between them. A program that means "the
// first row, if there is one" narrows before it calls.
var $refused = Reads::row($rows->first());
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0401]: expected `Core\Db\Row`, found `null|Core\Db\Row`
   --> case.nvs:53:27
%A
error: aborting due to 1 error
